WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.160
When is a language A language?

00:00:02.220 --> 00:00:04.230
What constitutes a language?

00:00:05.010 --> 00:00:09.450
And I, sort of, am thinking about
this question as I'm heading off on my

00:00:09.450 --> 00:00:12.900
trip to Denmark, Sweden, and Poland.

00:00:13.710 --> 00:00:16.770
Uh, and I've been practicing
my Danish and my Polish, my

00:00:16.770 --> 00:00:18.520
Swedish is already fairly good.

00:00:19.660 --> 00:00:24.210
And so then I think to myself,
well, Danish and Swedish and

00:00:24.210 --> 00:00:25.920
Norwegian are very, very close.

00:00:25.920 --> 00:00:29.670
They're kind of mutually comprehensible.

00:00:29.700 --> 00:00:32.250
The Danes have an easier
time understanding the

00:00:32.250 --> 00:00:33.810
Swedes than the Norwegians.

00:00:34.290 --> 00:00:37.710
The Swedes have a harder time
understanding the Danes, but essentially

00:00:38.010 --> 00:00:40.380
the vocabulary is very, very similar.

00:00:40.385 --> 00:00:42.660
The structure is very, very
similar, and yet they are

00:00:42.665 --> 00:00:45.420
recognized as separate languages.

00:00:46.190 --> 00:00:52.050
Now I move on to Polish, which I've been
essentially only listening to and reading,

00:00:52.110 --> 00:00:53.760
and I don't know how I'm gonna do there.

00:00:53.760 --> 00:00:55.980
I will certainly do some
videos when I'm in Poland.

00:00:56.910 --> 00:00:59.550
You know, in Denmark, I might do
some videos and you'll see that

00:00:59.550 --> 00:01:01.740
I'll still be speaking Swedish,
but they'll understand me.

00:01:01.740 --> 00:01:06.630
I, I've, I don't think I'll be pronouncing
Danish, but when I'm in Poland, I'm in

00:01:06.635 --> 00:01:08.550
a totally different language system.

00:01:08.790 --> 00:01:12.670
So if I'm gonna try to say anything,
I'm gonna have to say it in Polish.

00:01:13.440 --> 00:01:18.450
Now I know other Slavic languages,
but they're sufficiently different

00:01:18.720 --> 00:01:23.430
and, and no one suggests that Polish
is actually Czech or Ukrainian or.

00:01:24.000 --> 00:01:24.899
It's a separate language.

00:01:24.899 --> 00:01:29.789
There is no issue there whatsoever, but
these languages influence each other.

00:01:30.179 --> 00:01:36.360
So if you say take, uh, Czeck and
Slovak, now those are considered

00:01:36.360 --> 00:01:41.610
separate languages, but they're
so similar that Czecks and Slovaks

00:01:41.615 --> 00:01:45.449
have absolutely no difficulty
understanding, understanding each other.

00:01:45.660 --> 00:01:47.789
So why is that two separate languages?

00:01:48.000 --> 00:01:48.479
Question.

00:01:48.870 --> 00:01:49.290
All right.

00:01:49.740 --> 00:01:57.690
Uh, a similar situation, uh, occurs in,
in the Balkans where, okay, Slovenian is

00:01:58.260 --> 00:02:06.300
somewhat of an outlier, but, uh, Bosnian,
Serbian, Croatian are so similar that

00:02:06.780 --> 00:02:12.060
probably, you know, differences within
those countries can be as great as the

00:02:12.060 --> 00:02:14.010
differences between those languages.

00:02:14.310 --> 00:02:18.584
And yet there are some people who say,
well, I speak, you know, I'm a Serbian.

00:02:18.584 --> 00:02:20.265
I don't speak Croatian.

00:02:20.654 --> 00:02:25.095
Uh, but a majority of people there,
from what I've heard, agree that

00:02:25.095 --> 00:02:26.234
it's essentially one language.

00:02:26.535 --> 00:02:30.375
Although Serbian does, can use
Cyrillic alphabet, but also does

00:02:30.375 --> 00:02:31.665
use the Latin alphabet at times.

00:02:31.725 --> 00:02:34.214
I don't fully understand why
they use the one or the other.

00:02:34.725 --> 00:02:37.994
Um, also in the Balkans you
have Macedonian and Bulgarian

00:02:37.994 --> 00:02:38.954
that are very similar.

00:02:38.954 --> 00:02:42.885
Again, I don't speak either of those
languages, so, so you start to see

00:02:42.885 --> 00:02:50.804
where sort of the political situation
tends to determine whether a a, a form

00:02:50.804 --> 00:02:52.935
of a language is considered a language.

00:02:52.935 --> 00:02:55.065
In other words, is there a country there?

00:02:55.575 --> 00:03:00.135
Uh, but that's not necessarily clear
either because, you know, in Canada we

00:03:00.135 --> 00:03:04.364
have the people in Quebec and in New
Brunswick and elsewhere in Canada who

00:03:04.364 --> 00:03:06.435
speak French, but we have two languages.

00:03:06.435 --> 00:03:07.875
So it's a bilingual country.

00:03:07.964 --> 00:03:11.595
The the situation of Ukrainian,
there are people in Russia who don't

00:03:11.595 --> 00:03:15.015
want to admit that the Ukrainian
language exists when in fact...

00:03:15.644 --> 00:03:18.825
I don't know the full story of
the evolution of Ukrainian and,

00:03:19.155 --> 00:03:21.255
and the Belarusian language.

00:03:21.674 --> 00:03:25.545
I think they were essentially
the Ruthenian language, and that

00:03:25.545 --> 00:03:30.464
was one form of say, Eastern
Slavic languages and Russian.

00:03:30.464 --> 00:03:36.404
It was a sort of a different form and
the, uh, lexical distance, in other

00:03:36.404 --> 00:03:41.280
words, the vocabulary uh, as between,
you know, Russian and Ukrainian is

00:03:41.370 --> 00:03:46.890
actually more distant than say the
relationship between Ukrainian and Polish.

00:03:47.160 --> 00:03:51.150
Getting back to my Polish situation,
and of course there are all kinds of

00:03:51.240 --> 00:03:53.700
historical reasons why that's the case.

00:03:53.705 --> 00:03:57.900
If we look at, at Asia, for example,
there's 90 million speakers of

00:03:57.900 --> 00:04:01.440
Cantonese, yet Cantonese, is it
a language or is it a dialect?

00:04:01.680 --> 00:04:05.865
The interesting thing is that
most Cantonese speakers consider

00:04:05.925 --> 00:04:10.755
themselves to be Chinese and are not
that interested in having a separate

00:04:10.755 --> 00:04:12.675
language status for Cantonese.

00:04:13.005 --> 00:04:15.855
To the best of my knowledge,
some do, but a majority don't.

00:04:15.855 --> 00:04:19.935
The ones that I have met, they don't
make a big deal out of, of the fact

00:04:19.935 --> 00:04:21.495
that Cantonese is a separate language.

00:04:21.555 --> 00:04:22.935
They're happy to speak Cantonese.

00:04:22.940 --> 00:04:27.225
They have Cantonese radio, but they're
Chinese and they speak Chinese.

00:04:27.705 --> 00:04:32.835
Um, you know, in Taiwan you have this
Min Nan language, which is also spoken

00:04:32.835 --> 00:04:35.475
in the southern part of Fujian Province.

00:04:35.685 --> 00:04:39.885
That seems to be more recognized as a
separate language, although again, I

00:04:39.885 --> 00:04:44.895
can't, I'm not an expert, so maybe it's
just considered a dialect of, of Chinese.

00:04:45.045 --> 00:04:50.594
You often hear Chinese languages,
what I would consider to be languages,

00:04:50.594 --> 00:04:55.275
are considered dialects because
that's how the people who speak those

00:04:55.275 --> 00:04:57.165
languages seem to look upon them.

00:04:58.155 --> 00:05:03.435
From what I can tell, um, I'm not
knowledgeable about differences between

00:05:03.435 --> 00:05:08.565
Indonesian and, and, and Malaysian,
for example, or, you know, Thai and,

00:05:08.570 --> 00:05:11.685
and Laotian or any of those situations.

00:05:11.685 --> 00:05:12.945
I'm not at all familiar with them.

00:05:13.455 --> 00:05:15.615
Uh, so at what point is it a language?

00:05:15.645 --> 00:05:17.205
At what point is it a dialect?

00:05:17.344 --> 00:05:21.765
If you go back to Europe, of course,
uh, there used to be a lot more

00:05:21.765 --> 00:05:26.565
regional languages in France, in
Italy, in Germany, for example.

00:05:27.165 --> 00:05:33.615
And then gradually there was this
sort of centralizing trend in the

00:05:33.615 --> 00:05:38.505
19th century in particular as part
of the sort of national awakening.

00:05:38.505 --> 00:05:41.865
So the Germans gotta have one language,
the French have gotta have one language,

00:05:42.195 --> 00:05:47.685
and they and the Italians, and they ended
up essentially discouraging and in some

00:05:47.685 --> 00:05:54.735
cases depressing or even forbidding people
from speaking these regional languages.

00:05:55.155 --> 00:05:59.055
In Spain, the regional languages
seem to have survived better.

00:05:59.295 --> 00:06:06.525
So Catalan and uh, and Gallego, Galician,
uh, is still there as a regional

00:06:06.585 --> 00:06:08.355
language within the romance group.

00:06:08.355 --> 00:06:12.635
Of course, Basque  is a another
regional language, and even within

00:06:13.095 --> 00:06:18.045
the Catalan language, some people in
the Balearic Islands or Valencia say

00:06:18.045 --> 00:06:19.215
that their language is different.

00:06:19.215 --> 00:06:20.085
It's a different language.

00:06:20.085 --> 00:06:21.345
Or is it Catalan?

00:06:21.405 --> 00:06:22.125
Again, I don't know.

00:06:22.125 --> 00:06:23.715
I'm not in those discussions.

00:06:24.210 --> 00:06:28.020
But there is a sort of, sort
of subjective nature as to what

00:06:28.020 --> 00:06:29.820
we consider to be a language.

00:06:30.330 --> 00:06:36.270
Um, you know, I I, I have no familiarity
with Nahuatl, for example, in Mexico,

00:06:36.630 --> 00:06:39.850
but I understand that there are some
interesting situations with Nahuatl

00:06:39.875 --> 00:06:45.690
or Guaraní in Paraguay that they're
most closely related, sort of Native

00:06:45.690 --> 00:06:49.950
North or Native American language
may not be the one that's nearby, but

00:06:49.950 --> 00:06:51.720
sometimes there's one from further away.

00:06:52.050 --> 00:06:54.750
But they are certainly
recognized as languages.

00:06:54.900 --> 00:06:56.200
We have a case of Esperanto.

00:06:56.220 --> 00:07:01.230
There is no Esperanto country, but
people who speak Esperanto feel very

00:07:01.230 --> 00:07:03.000
strongly that it's an important language.

00:07:03.420 --> 00:07:09.840
So maybe the decision on whether something
is a, a language or not, or a dialect

00:07:09.870 --> 00:07:16.590
that should depend on what the speakers
of that language think and believe.

00:07:17.835 --> 00:07:21.315
I think if, if, uh, the speaker of a
certain language says, I speak language

00:07:21.344 --> 00:07:24.105
X, it's a language, it's not a dialect.

00:07:24.855 --> 00:07:26.085
I think we have to accept that.

00:07:26.655 --> 00:07:34.335
Uh, and if that is, then, if that gains
general acceptance, then not only is

00:07:34.335 --> 00:07:37.245
the speaker of that language saying
it's not a dialect, it's a language.

00:07:37.755 --> 00:07:41.325
But if we accept it, then it gains
general acceptance and then that then

00:07:41.325 --> 00:07:47.100
becomes a language and not a dialect,
but it's, it's a difficult situation.

00:07:47.219 --> 00:07:52.350
You know, people might refer to, say
the, uh, Haitian, uh, what do they

00:07:52.355 --> 00:07:57.840
call it, Creole, uh, sometimes the
term patois is used to describe a sort

00:07:57.840 --> 00:08:00.480
of, you know, uh, a hybrid language.

00:08:01.130 --> 00:08:05.610
But look at the languages of Europe
that, um, you know, emanated from,

00:08:05.610 --> 00:08:11.219
from Latin, uh, in France and
in, in, uh, Spain and so forth.

00:08:11.685 --> 00:08:15.735
The Iberian Peninsula, they must have
been some form of patois, even English,

00:08:16.005 --> 00:08:20.805
which is a, a hodgepodge mixture of,
I guess there's some Celtic words in

00:08:20.805 --> 00:08:25.395
there I don't know, but predominantly
Germanic words and, and words not so

00:08:25.395 --> 00:08:29.650
much from the Romans, it turns out, but
more from the Norman French and out of

00:08:29.650 --> 00:08:35.294
that evolution, patois, whatever you
want to call it uh, came the language

00:08:35.294 --> 00:08:37.034
that is known today as English.

00:08:37.304 --> 00:08:40.664
So the fact that a language is a
mixture of other languages or that

00:08:40.664 --> 00:08:47.235
is an sort of process of evolution,
none of that is, is disqualifying.

00:08:47.655 --> 00:08:49.515
None of that is strange.

00:08:49.545 --> 00:08:54.885
It's all a part of how languages
evolve and, uh, ultimately the decision

00:08:54.885 --> 00:09:00.690
on whether a patois or a Creole
or a dialect is a language or not

00:09:00.690 --> 00:09:03.420
becomes and remains quite subjective.

00:09:03.630 --> 00:09:06.870
So I look forward to lots of
comment from people who know a lot

00:09:06.870 --> 00:09:08.970
more about this subject than I do.

00:09:09.390 --> 00:09:10.200
Thank you for listening.

